# Jellyfinity v0.2.x–v0.3.0 specifications

Read only the assigned version. Each version is one complete, user-visible
music increment. All versions require focused changes, behavior tests, a
current changelog entry, and passing CI. A durable technical choice receives
an ADR. This arc intentionally remains music-only: movies, TV, sharing,
collaboration, and theme customization are out of scope.

## Big goal — dependable music without a connection

Make downloads feel like normal Jellyfinity music rather than a separate
mode. A person can deliberately keep songs, albums, artists, and playlists
on the device; play them through the normal queue when the server cannot be
reached; understand progress and storage use; and remove them only by an
explicit action. Downloads must survive app restarts and server-side deletion,
work at large-library scale, and never make remote music disappear.

The sequence below delivers value at every release. The small amount of
platform/download-engine investigation in v0.2.0 is release work only when it
ends in a usable track and album download experience; it is not a standalone
"prework" branch.

## v0.2.0 — Downloaded tracks and albums

**Goal:** Let a listener take individual tracks and whole albums with them and
play them reliably while offline.

**Required:**

- Add domain contracts for a download request, item state, progress, failure,
  local file reference, and explicit removal. Keep Jellyfin transport details,
  filesystem paths, and platform-worker details in infrastructure.
- Extend the local database with ordered, migratable download records and
  metadata sufficient to render and queue a local-only track without asking
  the server. Store downloaded audio outside disposable caches.
- Select and validate a replaceable Android+iOS download execution seam that
  can resume safely after an app/background interruption. It must support
  cancellation, retry, atomic completion, stale-session failure, and cleanup
  of a partial file. Record the choice in an ADR.
- Add Download / Remove download actions for a track and an album, including
  visible queued, in-progress, complete, paused/retryable, failed, and
  insufficient-storage states. Album actions show aggregate progress and do
  not conceal a failed track.
- Resolve a completed local file before a remote stream in playback while
  retaining the existing queue, playback failure, and availability semantics.
  A downloaded item remains playable when the server is unreachable.
- Keep artwork and persisted metadata usable where already cached; clearly
  distinguish unavailable remote-only items from downloaded/local-only ones.
- Test download state transitions, restart recovery, local-source preference,
  failed/cancelled cleanup, album aggregation, migration, and UI state. Manually
  validate background/interruption behavior on Android and iOS.

**Done when:** A user can download a track or album, see honest progress, close
the app, later play the completed music without a server connection, and remove
it deliberately without affecting its server copy.

## v0.2.1 — Downloadable playlists

**Goal:** Make a playlist a reliable offline listening set, including the
ability to keep it current when the server is available.

**Required:**

- Add a Download / Remove download action to playlist detail. Download its
  ordered tracks using the v0.2.0 engine, preserving playlist numbering and
  partial/unavailable-item behavior.
- Treat a playlist download as a durable membership snapshot: an already
  downloaded track is reused; duplicate requests do not duplicate files; a
  failed member remains visible and retryable rather than invalidating the
  entire playlist.
- On a user-requested refresh and when the playlist is opened online, reconcile
  membership against Jellyfin: queue new tracks, retain removed tracks only
  when another download owns them, and report what changed. Do not silently
  spend storage through background auto-sync in this version.
- Allow offline playback from the downloaded playlist detail and make its
  offline/partial state intelligible. Remote metadata refresh must never erase
  a locally completed file.
- Test deduplication, ordered snapshot playback, partial completion, changed
  playlist membership, deletion/reference ownership, and offline UI behavior.

**Stretch:** An explicit "download all available" recovery action for a
partially completed playlist.

**Done when:** A user can download a playlist, play its completed members in
order offline, refresh it deliberately online, and see precisely which tracks
still need attention.

## v0.2.2 — Artist downloads, download quality, and management

**Goal:** Let users manage a meaningful offline collection without losing
control of quality, network use, or device storage.

**Required:**

- Add artist-level download and removal, defined as the artist's browsable
  albums/tracks at the time of request. It uses the same ownership and
  deduplication rules as album and playlist downloads; it must never load an
  entire large artist/library into memory.
- Add a Downloads screen reachable from normal music navigation. It presents
  active work, completed collections, failed items, per-item retry/cancel,
  remove actions, and accurate aggregate storage usage.
- Persist a download-quality preference independent of streaming quality, with
  an explicit original/lossless option and only tiers Jellyfin can actually
  fulfill. A quality change applies to new/retried downloads; it does not
  silently replace existing files.
- Add Wi-Fi-only as an opt-in preference. If a constrained/no-network state
  blocks a request, retain it as a clearly paused request rather than reporting
  a permanent failure. Platform capability gaps must be disclosed in the UI
  and ADR.
- Make local-file ownership reference-counted (or an equivalently provable
  design) so removing one album, artist, or playlist does not delete a track
  still retained by another target.
- Test quality-to-source resolution, preference persistence, constrained-network
  pauses, large paged artist expansion, storage accounting, and shared-file
  retention/removal.

**Done when:** A user can download an artist, choose an intentional quality for
future downloads, see and control all download work in one place, and reclaim
space without accidentally removing music retained elsewhere.

## v0.2.3 — Offline library and recovery

**Goal:** Make downloaded music discoverable and trustworthy when the server is
unavailable or later changes.

**Required:**

- Add local-aware filtering to the existing music library and search so a user
  can find downloaded tracks, albums, artists, and playlists while offline.
  Queries are indexed, paginated, and bounded for the normal 130k-song scale.
- Preserve a downloaded item's display metadata, collection placement, and
  artwork reference independently of server presence. When a remote item is
  no longer returned, present it as **Only on this device** rather than delete
  it or misrepresent it as a remote failure.
- Reconcile downloaded records safely at session/server changes: never expose
  one account's local media to another account; do not delete local-only media
  as a side effect of sign-in, refresh, or server removal.
- Surface disk-write, corruption, expired authorization, and post-restart
  recovery failures through normalized download states with retry/re-auth paths.
  Recoverable work resumes only under the user's configured network policy.
- Add a storage-warning threshold before a request cannot complete; do not add
  automatic cleanup in this release.
- Test offline local search/filtering, account isolation, server-deleted local
  media, indexed paging at scale, state recovery, and every user-visible
  failure/empty/partial state.

**Done when:** With the server turned off, a user can find and play their
downloaded music through familiar screens; with the server changed or missing,
the app preserves downloaded content safely and explains its state.

## v0.3.0 — Offline music completion

**Goal:** Release a polished offline music experience that users can trust for
travel and daily listening.

**Required:**

- Audit every music entry point—library, album, artist, playlist, search,
  mini-player, Now Playing, queue, and relevant context menus—so download,
  offline, local-only, partial, and remote states are consistent and actionable.
- Support batch retry and explicit removal for failed/completed downloads, plus
  a clear confirmation that states the affected files and any shared retention.
  Removal never changes Jellyfin server media or playlist membership.
- Verify playback behavior across a mixed queue of local files, reachable
  streams, missing remote entries, and local-only entries. Preserve queue
  order, crossfade/normalization compatibility where supported by local files,
  and the existing non-destructive failure behavior.
- Harden lifecycle behavior: rapid network transitions, app termination during
  downloads, low storage, expired sessions, server deletion, and database/file
  mismatch must converge on an honest recoverable state without orphaned files
  or phantom completed downloads.
- Establish release-level Android and iOS device validation for background
  download, Wi-Fi policy, offline playback after restart, account switch,
  removal, and a representative large library. Add regression tests for the
  complete primary flows and update any ADRs/changelog entries required by the
  final architecture.

**Stretch:** Optional storage limit and automatic cleanup rules, but only if
they can provide a preview and never remove user-selected downloads silently.

**Done when:** Users can intentionally build, find, manage, and play a full
offline music collection with predictable quality, storage, recovery, and
server-independence behavior on Android and iOS.

## Non-goals for v0.2.0–v0.3.0

Movies, TV, video playback, video downloads, automatic cleanup, automatic
playlist synchronization, smart playlists, recommendations, social sharing,
collaboration, multi-server unified libraries, desktop/TV clients, theme
editing, and an offline-only app mode are outside this arc.

## Decisions to settle during implementation

- The default quality and default network policy must be chosen from measured
  storage and reliability behavior on representative devices. The intended
  safe starting point is original quality with Wi-Fi-only disabled, because it
  never silently changes what a user gets or blocks a requested download.
- The background-transfer dependency/implementation must be selected only after
  an Android+iOS resume/cancellation proof of behavior. If no candidate meets
  that bar, v0.2.0 may use a documented foreground-only implementation only if
  it still delivers a safe, useful download flow; otherwise the release is
  blocked rather than shipping unreliable downloads.
